---
import CalcPage from '../../components/CalcPage.astro';
import Field from '../../components/Field.astro';
---

<CalcPage
  title="Concentration and dilution"
  kicker="Concentration"
  lede="M1V1 = M2V2 is memorable once you see where it comes from. Fill in three of the four boxes, leave the fourth empty, and this solves it and tells you how much diluent to add."
  description="Solve M1V1 = M2V2 for any missing value, in percent, mg/mL, molarity or ratio strength, and find the volume of diluent to add."
>
  <form slot="form" id="calc">
    <div class="field-grid">
      <div class="field">
        <label for="concUnit">Concentration unit</label>
        <select id="concUnit" name="concUnit">
          <option value="percent">Percentage strength (%)</option>
          <option value="mgml">mg/mL</option>
          <option value="molar">Molarity (M)</option>
          <option value="ratio">Ratio strength (1 : X)</option>
        </select>
        <span class="hint">
          Applies to both strengths. The formula only needs the two to match
          each other, so any unit works as long as it is the same on both
          sides.
        </span>
      </div>
      <div class="field">
        <label for="volUnit">Volume unit</label>
        <select id="volUnit" name="volUnit">
          <option value="mL">Milliliters (mL)</option>
          <option value="L">Liters (L)</option>
        </select>
        <span class="hint">Applies to both volumes.</span>
      </div>
    </div>

    <fieldset>
      <legend>What you start with</legend>
      <div class="field-grid">
        <Field label="Starting strength (M1)" name="m1" placeholder="95" />
        <Field label="Starting volume (V1)" name="v1" placeholder="280" />
      </div>
    </fieldset>

    <fieldset>
      <legend>What you want to end up with</legend>
      <div class="field-grid">
        <Field label="Final strength (M2)" name="m2" placeholder="70" />
        <Field label="Final volume (V2)" name="v2" placeholder="leave blank" />
      </div>
    </fieldset>

    <p class="hint" style="margin-top:-0.6rem">
      Fill in three of the four boxes and leave the one you want empty. For a
      ratio strength, enter only the X, so 1 : 1000 goes in as 1000.
    </p>

    <div class="field-actions">
      <button class="btn btn--primary" type="submit">Solve</button>
      <button class="btn btn--quiet" type="reset">Clear</button>
    </div>

    <div class="field-actions" style="margin-top:0.9rem">
      <button
        class="btn btn--quiet btn--tiny"
        type="button"
        data-example='{"concUnit":"percent","volUnit":"mL","m1":"95","v1":"280","m2":"70","v2":""}'
      >
        Example: 70% alcohol from 95%
      </button>
      <button
        class="btn btn--quiet btn--tiny"
        type="button"
        data-example='{"concUnit":"percent","volUnit":"mL","m1":"10","v1":"100","m2":"2","v2":""}'
      >
        Example: how much water to add
      </button>
      <button
        class="btn btn--quiet btn--tiny"
        type="button"
        data-example='{"concUnit":"mgml","volUnit":"mL","m1":"250","v1":"","m2":"25","v2":"10"}'
      >
        Example: pediatric dose from stock
      </button>
      <button
        class="btn btn--quiet btn--tiny"
        type="button"
        data-example='{"concUnit":"ratio","volUnit":"mL","m1":"1000","v1":"1","m2":"","v2":"10"}'
      >
        Example: 1:1000 epinephrine
      </button>
    </div>
  </form>

  <div slot="concept">
    <h2>Nothing is added except diluent</h2>
    <p>
      When you dilute something, you do not touch the drug. You only give it
      more room. The 250 mg that was sitting in 1 mL of stock is still 250 mg
      after you add 9 mL of water, just spread through 10 mL instead of 1.
    </p>

    <p>
      That is the whole idea, and it is why the formula exists. Strength times
      volume is the amount of drug:
    </p>

    <pre class="formula">strength × volume  =  amount of drug</pre>

    <p>
      Since diluting cannot change the amount of drug, that product has to come
      out the same before and after. Write it both ways and set them equal:
    </p>

    <pre class="formula">M1 × V1  =  M2 × V2</pre>

    <p>
      M1 and V1 describe the stock you measure out. M2 and V2 describe the
      finished preparation. It is not a rule to memorize so much as a sentence:
      the drug you had is the drug you have.
    </p>

    <h3>Why the units barely matter</h3>
    <p>
      Both sides are the same kind of quantity, so anything that scales with
      strength cancels out. Percent, mg/mL, molarity: all of them work, and you
      never have to convert. The only requirement is that M1 and M2 are in the
      same unit as each other, and V1 and V2 are in the same unit as each
      other.
    </p>

    <div class="callout">
      <p>
        <strong>Ratio strength is the exception.</strong> A ratio strength runs
        backwards, because 1 : 1000 is weaker than 1 : 100. Turn it into a
        concentration first by dividing 1 by the X, then use that. Feeding 1000
        and 100 straight into the formula inverts the whole problem.
      </p>
    </div>

    <h3>The number the question actually wants</h3>
    <p>
      The formula hands you V2, the total finished volume. Exam questions
      almost never ask for that. They ask how much water, or syrup, or saline
      you add, and that is the difference:
    </p>

    <pre class="formula">diluent  =  V2 − V1</pre>

    <p>
      This matters at the bench too. You do not pour out 10 mL of anything. You
      measure 1 mL of stock, then add 9 mL of diluent to it.
    </p>

    <h3>The sanity check</h3>
    <p>
      A dilution makes the strength go <strong>down</strong> and the volume go
      <strong>up</strong>. Always. If your answer moved either one the other
      way, you have M1 and M2 swapped, so fix the setup rather than trusting
      the number.
    </p>

    <p>
      The same equation does describe concentrating a solution, by boiling
      water off, and this tool will still solve it. It just says so, because on
      a dilution problem a rising strength is nearly always a mistake.
    </p>
  </div>
</CalcPage>

<script>
  import { setupCalculator } from '../../scripts/calc.js';
  import { solveConcentrationDilution } from '../../scripts/solvers/concentration-dilution.js';

  setupCalculator({
    form: '#calc',
    solve: solveConcentrationDilution,
  });
</script>
